    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Preventing Perinatal Transmission",
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "Get an HIV test. If you know you have HIV, you can take steps to lower your risk of perinatal transmission.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/hiv/conditioninfo/prevent",
    "title": "How can HIV be prevented? | NICHD - Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Preventing Perinatal Transmission",
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "If you have HIV, take anti-HIV drugs for yourself and your child. You should take anti-HIV drugs during pregnancy, labor, and birth, and your child should take them for the first weeks of life.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/hiv/conditioninfo/prevent",
    "title": "How can HIV be prevented? | NICHD - Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Preventing Perinatal Transmission",
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "If you have HIV, avoid breastfeeding. HIV can pass to your child through breast milk. If you live in the United States or another country with safe water, formula feeding is best for prevention of HIV.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/hiv/conditioninfo/prevent",
