    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/nsaids/",
    "title": "NSAIDs\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Food and alcohol",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The leaflet that comes with your medicine should say whether you need to avoid any particular foods or drinks. Ask your pharmacist or doctor if you're not sure.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/nsaids/",
    "title": "NSAIDs\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Food and alcohol",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "For information about a specific medicine, check the product information about medicines on the GOV.UK website.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/nsaids/",
    "title": "NSAIDs\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Food and alcohol",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Tablets or capsules should normally be swallowed whole, without chewing, and taken with water or food to stop them upsetting your stomach.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/nsaids/",
    "title": "NSAIDs\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Food and alcohol",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "It's usually safe to drink alcohol while taking NSAIDs, but drinking alcohol excessively may irritate your stomach.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/nsaids/",
    "title": "NSAIDs\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Overdoses of NSAIDs",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Taking too much of an NSAID can be dangerous. This is known as taking an overdose.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/nsaids/",
    "title": "NSAIDs\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Overdoses of NSAIDs",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Contact your GP or NHS 111 for advice immediately if you take too much of your medicine.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/nsaids/",
    "title": "NSAIDs\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Overdoses of NSAIDs",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Call 999 for an ambulance immediately if you or someone else experiences serious effects of an overdose, such as fits (seizures), breathing difficulties, or loss of consciousness.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/nsaids/",
    "title": "NSAIDs\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Alternatives to NSAIDs",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "As NSAIDs can cause troublesome side effects, alternatives are often recommended first.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/nsaids/",
    "title": "NSAIDs\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Alternatives to NSAIDs",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The main alternative for pain relief is paracetamol, which is available over the counter and is safe for most people to take.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/nsaids/",
    "title": "NSAIDs\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Alternatives to NSAIDs",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "NSAID creams and gels that you rub into your skin may be worth trying first if you have muscle or joint pain in a particular part of your body, as they tend to have fewer side effects than tablets or capsules.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/nsaids/",
    "title": "NSAIDs\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Alternatives to NSAIDs",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Your doctor may also be able to recommend different medicines and therapies depending on the health problem you have.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "We're supposed to be keeping an eye on how much we drink, but how many of us really know what a unit of alcohol is?",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "With so many different drinks and glass sizes, from shots to pints – not to mention bottles – it's easy to get confused about how many units are in your drink.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The idea of counting alcohol units was first introduced in the UK in 1987 to help people keep track of their drinking.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "One unit equals 10ml or 8g of pure alcohol, which is around the amount of alcohol the average adult can process in an hour.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "This means that within an hour there should be, in theory, little or no alcohol left in the blood of an adult, although this will vary from person to person.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The number of units in a drink is based on the size of the drink, as well as its alcohol strength.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "For example, a pint of strong lager contains 3 units of alcohol, whereas the same volume of lower-strength lager has just over 2 units.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "men and women are advised not to drink more than 14 units a week on a regular basis",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "spread your drinking over 3 or more days if you regularly drink as much as 14 units a week",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "14 units is equivalent to 6 pints of average-strength beer or 10 small glasses of lower-strength wine.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Calculating units",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Using units is a simpler way of representing a drink's alcohol content – usually expressed by the standard measure alcohol by volume (ABV).",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Calculating units",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "ABV is a measure of the amount of pure alcohol as a percentage of the total volume of liquid in a drink.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Calculating units",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "You can find the ABV on the labels of cans and bottles, sometimes written as \"vol\" or \"alcohol volume\", or you can ask bar staff about particular drinks.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Calculating units",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "For example, wine that says \"12% ABV\" or \"alcohol volume 12%\" means 12% of the volume of that drink is pure alcohol.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Calculating units",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "You can work out how many units there are in any drink by multiplying the total volume of a drink (in ml) by its ABV (measured as a percentage) and dividing the result by 1,000.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Calculating units",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "For example, to work out the number of units in a pint (568ml) of strong lager (ABV 5.2%):",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calculating-alcohol-units/",
    "title": "Alcohol units - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Drinks and units",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "*Gin, rum, vodka, whisky, tequila and sambuca. Large (35ml) single measures of spirits are 1.4 units.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Realising you have a problem with alcohol is the first big step to getting help.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "A good place to start is with a GP. Try to be accurate and honest about how much you drink and any problems it may be causing you.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "If you have become dependent on alcohol, you will have found it difficult to fully control your drinking in some way.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "So you'll probably need some help either to cut down and control your drinking or stop completely, and also some plans to maintain the improvement after that.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The GP may suggest different types of assessment and support options available to you, such as from local community alcohol services.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "You can also ask about any free local support groups and other alcohol counselling that may suit you.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "If you have become physically dependent and need to stop drinking completely, stopping overnight could be harmful.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "You should get advice about this and about any medicine you may need to do this safely.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The sorts of mild or moderate withdrawal symptoms that suggest you may need medicine include:",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Staying healthy and in control",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Cutting down or stopping drinking is usually just the beginning, and most people will need some degree of help or a long-term plan to stay in control or completely alcohol free.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Staying healthy and in control",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Getting the right support can be crucial to maintaining control in the future. Only relying on family, friends or carers for this often is not enough.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Staying healthy and in control",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Ask a GP or alcohol service about what longer-term support is available in your area.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Staying healthy and in control",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Self-help or mutual aid groups (groups such as AA or SMART Recovery groups) are accessible in most areas.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Useful contacts for alcohol problems",
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "Drinkline is the national alcohol helpline. If you're worried about your own or someone else's drinking, you can call this free helpline in complete confidence. Call 0300 123 1110 (weekdays 9am to 8pm, weekends 11am to 4pm).",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Useful contacts for alcohol problems",
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) is a free self-help group. Its \"12 step\" programme involves getting sober with the help of regular support groups.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Useful contacts for alcohol problems",
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "Al-Anon Family Groups offer support and understanding to the families and friends of problem drinkers, whether they're still drinking or not. Alateen is part of Al-Anon and can be attended by 12 to 17-year-olds who are affected by another person's drinking, usually a parent.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Useful contacts for alcohol problems",
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "We Are With You is a UK-wide treatment agency that helps individuals, families and communities manage the effects of drug and alcohol misuse.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Useful contacts for alcohol problems",
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "Adfam is a national charity working with families affected by drugs and alcohol. Adfam operates an online message board and local support groups.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Useful contacts for alcohol problems",
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "The National Association for Children of Alcoholics (Nacoa) provides a free, confidential telephone and email helpline for children of alcohol-dependent parents and others concerned about their welfare. Call 0800 358 3456 for the Nacoa helpline.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Useful contacts for alcohol problems",
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "SMART Recovery groups help people decide whether they have a problem, build up their motivation to change, and offer a set of proven tools and techniques to support recovery.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Useful contacts for alcohol problems",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Caring for an alcoholic? Find out where you can get support on the Carers Trust website.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Alcohol detoxification",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Most people receive support to stop drinking and recovery support in the community.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Alcohol detoxification",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "If you need medicine to help you stop drinking, it can often be taken at home or when attending a local service daily.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Alcohol detoxification",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "But some people will need a short stay in a medically supported unit so they can receive safe treatment of their withdrawal symptoms or other problems.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Alcohol detoxification",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "This may be in an NHS inpatient unit or a medically supported residential service, depending on your situation and the assessed medical need.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Intensive rehabilitation",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Some people are assessed as needing intensive rehabilitation and recovery support for a period after they stop drinking completely, either through attending a programme of intensive support in their local community or by attending a residential rehabilitation service.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Intensive rehabilitation",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "This type of intensive treatment is usually reserved for people with medium or high levels of alcohol dependence, and those who have received other forms of help previously that have not been successful.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Intensive rehabilitation",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Local authorities are responsible for alcohol treatment services. Intensive residential rehabilitation may require an additional assessment process to determine if there is funding for this.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/alcohol-support/",
    "title": "Alcohol support - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Intensive rehabilitation",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "It's also possible to pay for residential rehabilitation privately. Medical insurance companies may fund this for a certain period.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calories-in-alcohol/",
    "title": "Calories in alcohol - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Did you know a standard glass of wine can contain up to 158 calories, and some pints of stronger lager can contain up to 222 calories? So, if you're trying to lose weight you need to think about what you are drinking as well as what you are eating.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calories-in-alcohol/",
    "title": "Calories in alcohol - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "general",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Drinking 4 bottles of 12% strength wine a month add up to a yearly consumption of up to 32,400kcal. Drinking 5 pints of 5.2% strength lager each week add up to 57,720kcal in a year.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calories-in-alcohol/",
    "title": "Calories in alcohol - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Liquid calories",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "You can find out the calorie content of other drinks using unit and calorie calculator on the Alcohol Change UK website.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calories-in-alcohol/",
    "title": "Calories in alcohol - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Weight gain",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Wine, beer, cider, spirits and many more of our favourite drinks are made from natural starch and sugar. Fermentation (and distillation for certain drinks) is used to produce the alcohol content.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/alcohol-advice/calories-in-alcohol/",
    "title": "Calories in alcohol - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Weight gain",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "This is why alcohol contains lots of calories – 7 calories per gram, which is almost as many as a gram of fat. Plus, additional calories can be in added mixer drinks, such as cola or tonic water.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
