  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/acne/",
    "title": "Acne\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Why do I have acne?",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Acne is most commonly linked to the changes in hormone levels during puberty, but can start at any age.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/acne/",
    "title": "Acne\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Why do I have acne?",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Certain hormones cause the grease-producing glands next to hair follicles in the skin to produce larger amounts of oil (abnormal sebum).",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/acne/",
    "title": "Acne\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Why do I have acne?",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "This abnormal sebum changes the activity of a usually harmless skin bacterium called P. acnes, which becomes more aggressive and causes inflammation and pus.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/acne/",
    "title": "Acne\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Why do I have acne?",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The hormones also thicken the inner lining of the hair follicle, causing blockage of the pores. Cleaning the skin does not help to remove this blockage.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/acne/",
    "title": "Acne\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Other possible causes",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Acne is known to run in families. If both your mother and father had acne, it's likely that you'll also have acne.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/acne/",
    "title": "Acne\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Other possible causes",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Hormonal changes, such as those that occur during the menstrual cycle or pregnancy, can also lead to episodes of acne in women.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/acne/",
    "title": "Acne\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Who's affected?",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Acne is very common in teenagers and younger adults. About 95% of people aged 11 to 30 are affected by acne to some extent.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/acne/",
    "title": "Acne\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Who's affected?",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Acne is most common in girls from the ages of 14 to 17, and in boys from the ages of 16 to 19.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
  {
    "source": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/acne/",
    "title": "Acne\n - NHS",
    "category": "clinical",
    "section": "Who's affected?",
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Most people have acne on and off for several years before their symptoms start to improve as they get older.",
    "keyword": "Vaginitis"
  },
