      {
        "question": "What is vaginitis?",
        "answer": "Vaginitis is inflammation of the vagina, often accompanied by discharge, itching, burning, and odor. It is one of the most common reasons women seek gynecological care, accounting for approximately 10 million office visits per year in the United States. Vaginitis can result from infection (bacterial, fungal, or parasitic), hormonal changes, allergic reactions, or chemical irritants. The three most common types are bacterial vaginosis (BV), vulvovaginal candidiasis (VVC, yeast infection), and trichomoniasis. Accurate diagnosis is essential because the treatments differ significantly between types."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are the types of vaginitis?",
        "answer": "The main types of vaginitis include: (1) Bacterial vaginosis (BV) — the most common type (40-50% of cases), caused by an overgrowth of anaerobic bacteria replacing normal Lactobacillus flora; (2) Vulvovaginal candidiasis (VVC) — caused by Candida species (most commonly Candida albicans), accounting for 20-25% of cases; (3) Trichomoniasis — a sexually transmitted infection caused by the protozoan Trichomonas vaginalis, accounting for 15-20% of cases; (4) Atrophic vaginitis (genitourinary syndrome of menopause) — caused by estrogen deficiency in postmenopausal women; (5) Aerobic vaginitis — caused by aerobic bacteria such as Streptococcus agalactiae and Staphylococcus aureus; (6) Irritant or allergic vaginitis — caused by soaps, detergents, spermicides, latex, or feminine hygiene products."
      },
      {
        "question": "What causes vaginitis?",
        "answer": "Vaginitis has multiple causes depending on the type: Bacterial vaginosis results from disruption of normal vaginal flora with overgrowth of anaerobes (Gardnerella vaginalis, Prevotella, Mobiluncus, Mycoplasma hominis); Vulvovaginal candidiasis is caused by Candida albicans (85-90% of cases) or non-albicans species (Candida glabrata, Candida tropicalis); Trichomoniasis is caused by the flagellated protozoan Trichomonas vaginalis, transmitted sexually; Atrophic vaginitis results from decreased estrogen production after menopause, oophorectomy, or during breastfeeding; Irritant vaginitis is triggered by chemical irritants including soaps, douches, spermicides, lubricants, latex condoms, or tight synthetic clothing."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are the symptoms of vaginitis?",
        "answer": "Symptoms vary by type but commonly include: abnormal vaginal discharge (change in color, consistency, or amount); vaginal itching, burning, or irritation; unpleasant or unusual vaginal odor; pain or discomfort during urination (dysuria); pain during sexual intercourse (dyspareunia); and vulvar redness or swelling. Bacterial vaginosis typically causes a thin, grey-white discharge with a characteristic fishy odor, especially after intercourse. Candidiasis causes a thick, white, cottage cheese-like discharge with intense itching and vulvar redness. Trichomoniasis causes a frothy, yellow-green discharge with a foul odor, itching, and sometimes cervical petechiae ('strawberry cervix'). Atrophic vaginitis causes vaginal dryness, burning, and pale watery discharge without odor."
      },
      {
        "question": "How is vaginitis diagnosed?",
        "answer": "Diagnosis involves clinical history, physical examination, and laboratory testing. Key investigations include: vaginal pH measurement (normal pH 3.8-4.5; BV and trichomoniasis elevate pH > 4.5; candidiasis pH is typically normal); microscopy of vaginal wet mount (saline preparation for clue cells in BV and motile trichomonads; KOH preparation for candidal pseudohyphae); whiff test (addition of KOH to discharge releases fishy amine odor in BV); vaginal swab culture (for Candida species, particularly in recurrent or treatment-resistant cases); and NAAT (nucleic acid amplification test) for Trichomonas vaginalis (most sensitive). The Amsel criteria (3 of 4: homogeneous discharge, pH > 4.5, positive whiff test, clue cells > 20%) or Nugent score (Gram stain) are used to diagnose BV."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is bacterial vaginosis (BV) and how is it treated?",
        "answer": "Bacterial vaginosis (BV) is the most common vaginal infection in women of reproductive age, caused by replacement of normal Lactobacillus-dominant flora with a polymicrobial overgrowth of anaerobic bacteria. It is characterized by a fishy-smelling, thin, grey-white discharge and vaginal pH > 4.5. BV is associated with sexual activity but is not classified as a classic STI. Treatment options include: Metronidazole 500mg orally twice daily for 7 days (first-line); Metronidazole 0.75% gel intravaginally once daily for 5 days; or Clindamycin 2% cream intravaginally at bedtime for 7 days. Alcohol should be avoided during and 24-48 hours after oral metronidazole. Partner treatment is not routinely recommended as it does not reduce recurrence rates."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is vulvovaginal candidiasis (yeast infection) and how is it treated?",
        "answer": "Vulvovaginal candidiasis (VVC) is a fungal infection caused by Candida species, most commonly Candida albicans. It presents with intense vulvovaginal itching, burning, a thick white cottage cheese-like discharge, and vulvar erythema and edema. It is not sexually transmitted. Predisposing factors include antibiotic use, pregnancy, diabetes, immunosuppression, high-dose estrogen contraceptives, and tight synthetic clothing. Treatment for uncomplicated VVC: topical azoles (clotrimazole, miconazole, butoconazole) for 1-7 days, or oral fluconazole 150mg single dose. For severe or recurrent VVC (4 or more episodes per year): fluconazole 150mg every 72 hours for 3 doses (induction), then weekly for 6 months (maintenance). Non-albicans candidiasis may require boric acid 600mg intravaginal suppositories for 14 days."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is trichomoniasis and how is it treated?",
        "answer": "Trichomoniasis is the most common non-viral sexually transmitted infection worldwide, caused by the flagellated protozoan Trichomonas vaginalis. It presents with a frothy, yellow-green, malodorous vaginal discharge, vulvovaginal itching and irritation, dysuria, and dyspareunia. Up to 70% of infected women and most infected men are asymptomatic. Diagnosis is by wet mount microscopy (motile trichomonads) or NAAT (most sensitive). Treatment: Metronidazole 2g orally as a single dose (first-line) or 500mg twice daily for 7 days (preferred in women with HIV); Tinidazole 2g orally as a single dose (alternative). Sexual partners must be treated simultaneously to prevent reinfection. Alcohol should be avoided during treatment and for 72 hours after tinidazole. Test of cure is recommended 3 months after treatment."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is atrophic vaginitis and how is it treated?",
        "answer": "Atrophic vaginitis, now termed genitourinary syndrome of menopause (GSM), is caused by estrogen deficiency leading to thinning, drying, and inflammation of vaginal and vulvar tissues. It affects up to 50% of postmenopausal women and can also occur during breastfeeding or after oophorectomy. Symptoms include vaginal dryness, burning, itching, pale watery discharge, dyspareunia, urinary urgency and frequency, and recurrent UTIs. Treatment options include: vaginal moisturizers and lubricants (first-line non-hormonal options); low-dose topical vaginal estrogen (cream, ring, or suppository — preferred as minimal systemic absorption); ospemifene (oral SERM for dyspareunia); and systemic hormone replacement therapy (HRT) for women with other menopausal symptoms. Regular sexual activity and vaginal dilators help maintain vaginal health."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can vaginitis be sexually transmitted?",
        "answer": "It depends on the type. Trichomoniasis is a classic sexually transmitted infection (STI) caused by Trichomonas vaginalis and is always transmitted through sexual contact — partner treatment is mandatory. Bacterial vaginosis is associated with sexual activity and new sexual partners but is not classified as a traditional STI; it can occur in sexually inactive women and partner treatment does not consistently reduce recurrence. Vulvovaginal candidiasis is generally not considered an STI; it is caused by overgrowth of endogenous Candida flora and is not reliably prevented by condom use, though sexual transmission can occasionally occur. Atrophic and irritant vaginitis are not sexually transmitted."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are the risk factors for vaginitis?",
        "answer": "Risk factors vary by type. For bacterial vaginosis: new or multiple sexual partners, female-to-female sexual contact, douching, lack of condom use, IUD use, and reduced Lactobacillus flora. For vulvovaginal candidiasis: recent antibiotic use (disrupts protective flora), pregnancy, uncontrolled diabetes, immunosuppression (HIV, corticosteroids), high-estrogen oral contraceptives, tight synthetic underwear, and hot humid environments. For trichomoniasis: multiple sexual partners, inconsistent condom use, history of STIs, and lower socioeconomic status. For atrophic vaginitis: menopause, surgical or premature ovarian failure, breastfeeding, and tamoxifen use. For irritant vaginitis: use of scented products, douching, latex sensitivity, and spermicide use."
