      {
        "question": "How is recurrent vaginitis managed?",
        "answer": "Recurrent vaginitis requires identification of the underlying cause and long-term management strategies. For recurrent BV (3 or more episodes per year): extended metronidazole gel twice weekly for 4-6 months after initial treatment; boric acid 600mg intravaginal suppositories for 21 days; or consideration of Lactobacillus vaginal probiotics. For recurrent VVC (4 or more episodes per year): confirm diagnosis with culture; exclude predisposing factors (diabetes, immunosuppression); induction with fluconazole 150mg every 72 hours for 3 doses, then weekly maintenance for 6 months. For recurrent trichomoniasis: confirm partner treatment and re-exposure; test for metronidazole resistance; consider tinidazole 2g daily for 5 days. General measures include avoiding douching, using unscented products, wearing breathable cotton underwear, and maintaining good glycemic control in diabetic women."
