        "question": "Can vaginitis affect pregnancy?",
        "answer": "Yes. Vaginitis during pregnancy can have significant maternal and fetal consequences. Bacterial vaginosis in pregnancy is associated with preterm labor, preterm premature rupture of membranes (PPROM), low birth weight, postpartum endometritis, and chorioamnionitis. Routine screening for BV in high-risk pregnant women (previous preterm birth) is recommended. Trichomoniasis in pregnancy is associated with preterm delivery and low birth weight; treatment with metronidazole is safe after the first trimester. Vulvovaginal candidiasis is more common in pregnancy due to elevated estrogen and glycogen levels; topical azoles are preferred (oral fluconazole should be avoided in pregnancy due to potential teratogenicity at high doses). Atrophic vaginitis does not occur during pregnancy due to high estrogen levels."
