      {
        "question": "How can vaginitis be prevented?",
        "answer": "Prevention strategies include: avoiding douching (disrupts natural vaginal flora and pH); using unscented soaps and avoiding perfumed feminine hygiene products; wearing breathable cotton underwear and avoiding tight synthetic clothing; wiping front to back after using the toilet; using condoms consistently to reduce STI-related vaginitis; avoiding unnecessary antibiotic use; maintaining good glycemic control in diabetic women; completing full courses of treatment and ensuring partner treatment for trichomoniasis; using water-based lubricants; and avoiding prolonged moisture (changing out of wet swimwear promptly). Probiotic supplements containing Lactobacillus rhamnosus and Lactobacillus reuteri show promising evidence for maintaining vaginal flora and preventing recurrent BV and candidiasis."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the difference between vaginitis and vaginosis?",
        "answer": "Vaginitis refers to inflammation of the vagina, which typically involves symptoms such as itching, burning, redness, and discharge — implying an inflammatory response. Vaginosis (specifically bacterial vaginosis) refers to a dysbiosis or imbalance of the vaginal microbiome without significant inflammation — hence the suffix '-osis' (condition) rather than '-itis' (inflammation). In bacterial vaginosis, the normal Lactobacillus-dominant flora is replaced by anaerobic bacteria, but the vaginal epithelium does not show significant inflammatory changes (few white blood cells on microscopy), which distinguishes it from true vaginitis. In clinical practice, the terms are often used interchangeably, but the distinction is important for accurate diagnosis and treatment."
